{
  "answer_text": "The evidence indicates that retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) reduces the hallucination rate of LLM-generated answers in several specific studies, though results vary by benchmark and metric. \n\nIn studies specifically measuring hallucination rate or frequency, RAG consistently showed a reduction compared to parametric-only or vanilla baselines:\n*   On LawFactQA, the retrieval-augmented model had a hallucination rate of 17.8%, compared to 29.5% for the parametric-only model [S001, 1932-2086].\n*   On PopCultureQA, the RAG pipeline had a hallucination rate of 18.3%, compared to 21.5% for the vanilla LLM [S006, 2189-2326].\n*   On SciFactBench, the retrieval-augmented generator had a hallucination rate of 6.3%, compared to 20.2% for the vanilla LLM [S008, 2598-2656].\n*   On HistoryQuest, the retrieval-augmented generator had a hallucination frequency of 5.1%, compared to 10.3% for the no-retrieval baseline [S016, 2303-2484].\n*   On an unnamed main benchmark in another study, the retrieve-then-read model had a hallucination rate of 16.3%, compared to 20.8% for the vanilla LLM [S018, 2705-2760].\n*   On TriviaCheck, the retrieval-augmented model had a hallucination rate of 32.3%, compared to 38.1% for the vanilla LLM [S010, 2143-2285].\n\nHowever, other studies using different metrics (such as fact-level accuracy or answer accuracy) or specific benchmarks reported that RAG did not measurably reduce hallucinations or even increased them:\n*   On OpenFactQA, the configuration that looks up supporting documents had a share of answers with unsupported statements of 39.9% (0.399), compared to 39.1% (391/1000) for the generator without document access, leading to the conclusion that RAG does not measurably reduce hallucinated content [S007, 2721-3019].\n*   On NewsTruthQA, the variant that reads retrieved evidence first had a share of answers with unsupported statements of 29.2%, compared to 19.3% (0.193) for the model answering from its own parameters, indicating RAG increased hallucinated content [S012, 2463-2752].\n*   Several studies measuring fact-level accuracy or answer accuracy found that RAG did not make answers measurably more accurate or made them less accurate, though these metrics are distinct from hallucination rate [S003, 3089-3145; S004, 2568-3143; S009, 2474-2874; S013, 3173-3242; S014, 1788-2254; S019, 2114-2715; S020, 2556-2737].\n\nOverall, while RAG is associated with reduced hallucination rates in multiple dedicated hallucination studies [S001, 66-412; S006, 72-583; S008, 57-353; S016, 89-607; S018, 3459-3739], the effect is not universal across all benchmarks and metrics [S007, 65-626; S012, 56-607].",
